export default "# Docker and Containers: Container Health and Delivery\n\n# PART 1 OF 4: UNDERSTANDING CONTAINER HEALTH\n\n## 1. Introduction to Container Health\n\nIn the previous lesson, we learned how to create Docker images using Dockerfiles and build containers from those images.\n\nHowever, successfully starting a container does not necessarily mean that the application inside it is working correctly.\n\nImagine deploying a marketplace API inside a Docker container.\n\nDocker reports:\n\n```text\nContainer started successfully.\n```\n\nBut when users attempt to access the API, they receive errors.\n\nWhat could have happened?\n\n- The application may have crashed internally.\n- The database connection may have failed.\n- The application may be running but unable to process requests.\n- The application may be waiting for another service.\n- The application may be responding too slowly.\n\nThis is why container health monitoring is important.\n\n**Container health** is the ability to determine whether an application running inside a container is functioning correctly and is ready to perform its intended tasks.\n\nWe need to distinguish between three conditions:\n\n| Condition | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Starting | The container has been created and its main process is starting. |\n| Running | The container's main process is still running. |\n| Healthy | The configured health check is passing. |\n| Unhealthy | The configured health check has repeatedly failed. |\n| Exited | The container's main process has stopped. |\n\nAn important distinction:\n\nA container can be running but unhealthy.\n\nFor example, a Node.js API might still be running while its database connection is broken.\n\nDocker knows that the process is running, but it cannot automatically know whether the API can serve users correctly.\n\nHealth checks help solve this problem.\n\n## 2. Understanding Docker Container States\n\nDocker maintains information about the lifecycle of each container.\n\nTo list running containers:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `docker` invokes the Docker command-line interface.\n- `ps` lists running containers.\n- The output includes container IDs, image names, commands, status, ports and names.\n\nTo list all containers, including stopped ones:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps -a\n```\n\nThe `-a` option means all containers.\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nCONTAINER ID   IMAGE          STATUS\na123456789     marketplace    Up 10 minutes\nb234567890     redis          Exited (0) 2 hours ago\nc345678901     nginx          Up 5 minutes\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- `marketplace` is running.\n- `redis` has stopped with exit code `0`.\n- `nginx` is running.\n\nAn exit code of `0` generally indicates successful completion. A non-zero exit code generally indicates an error or unsuccessful termination.\n\nTo examine a container's state:\n\n```bash\ndocker inspect marketplace-api\n```\n\nThis returns detailed JSON information about the container.\n\nTo display only its state:\n\n```bash\ndocker inspect --format='{{.State.Status}}' marketplace-api\n```\n\nTo display its health status:\n\n```bash\ndocker inspect --format='{{.State.Health.Status}}' marketplace-api\n```\n\nThe second command requires the container to have a configured health check.\n\n## 3. What Is a Docker Health Check?\n\nA Docker health check is a command that Docker executes periodically to determine whether the application inside a container is functioning as expected.\n\nA health check can test:\n\n- Whether an HTTP endpoint responds.\n- Whether a service is accepting connections.\n- Whether a database is responding.\n- Whether an application process is performing a basic operation.\n\nFor example, a marketplace API could expose:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000/health\n```\n\nThe endpoint might return:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"healthy\",\n  \"service\": \"marketplace-api\"\n}\n```\n\nDocker can periodically request this endpoint and examine whether it responds successfully.\n\n### Health check instructions in Dockerfile\n\nDocker provides a `HEALTHCHECK` instruction.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=10s --retries=3 \\\n  CMD node -e \"fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/health').then(r => process.exit(r.ok ? 0 : 1)).catch(() => process.exit(1))\"\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nLet's understand the important lines.\n\n**Line 1:**\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n```\n\nSelects a Node.js 22 Alpine image as the starting point.\n\n**Line 2:**\n\n```dockerfile\nWORKDIR /app\n```\n\nSets `/app` as the working directory inside the container.\n\n**Line 3:**\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY package*.json ./\n```\n\nCopies the package manifest and lock file into the container.\n\n**Line 4:**\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm ci\n```\n\nInstalls dependencies using the lock file.\n\n**Line 5:**\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY . .\n```\n\nCopies the remaining application files.\n\n**Line 6:**\n\n```dockerfile\nEXPOSE 3000\n```\n\nDocuments that the application uses port 3000. It does not publish the port to the host.\n\n**Health check:**\n\n```dockerfile\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=10s --retries=3 \\\n  CMD node -e \"fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/health').then(r => process.exit(r.ok ? 0 : 1)).catch(() => process.exit(1))\"\n```\n\nThis defines the health check.\n\nIts settings mean:\n\n| Setting | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `--interval=30s` | Run the health check every 30 seconds. |\n| `--timeout=5s` | Allow up to 5 seconds for a check to complete. |\n| `--start-period=10s` | Give the application an initial startup period. |\n| `--retries=3` | Mark the container unhealthy after three consecutive failed checks. |\n| `CMD` | Specifies the command Docker executes. |\n\nThe JavaScript command uses Node.js's built-in `fetch()` function to request the health endpoint.\n\nIf the response is successful, the process exits with code `0`.\n\nIf the response is unsuccessful or the request fails, it exits with code `1`.\n\n**Final line:**\n\n```dockerfile\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nStarts the Node.js application.\n\nThe health check only works correctly if the application actually provides the `/health` endpoint.\n\nIt also assumes the Node.js version supports the built-in `fetch()` function.\n\n## 4. Creating a Health Endpoint\n\nLet's create a simple health endpoint for our marketplace API.\n\nExample using Express:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require(\"express\");\n\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get(\"/health\", (req, res) => {\n    res.status(200).json({\n        status: \"healthy\",\n        service: \"marketplace-api\"\n    });\n});\n\napp.listen(3000, \"0.0.0.0\", () => {\n    console.log(\"Server running on port 3000\");\n});\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n**Import Express:**\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require(\"express\");\n```\n\nLoads the Express framework.\n\n**Create the application:**\n\n```javascript\nconst app = express();\n```\n\nCreates an Express application instance.\n\n**Create the health endpoint:**\n\n```javascript\napp.get(\"/health\", (req, res) => {\n```\n\nDefines an HTTP GET endpoint at `/health`.\n\nWhen Docker requests this URL, Express executes the callback.\n\n**Return a successful response:**\n\n```javascript\nres.status(200).json({\n    status: \"healthy\",\n    service: \"marketplace-api\"\n});\n```\n\nThe application returns HTTP status `200` and a JSON response.\n\n**Start the server:**\n\n```javascript\napp.listen(3000, \"0.0.0.0\", () => {\n```\n\nStarts the application on port 3000.\n\nThe address `0.0.0.0` allows the application to listen on all available network interfaces inside the container.\n\nThis is important because binding only to `127.0.0.1` can prevent access through the container's published network port.\n\n### Important distinction: Basic health versus dependency health\n\nOur current endpoint only proves that Express can respond.\n\nIt does not prove that MongoDB is working.\n\nA more useful health system can have separate endpoints:\n\n| Endpoint | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `/health` | Checks whether the application process can respond. |\n| `/ready` | Checks whether the application can accept normal traffic. |\n| `/live` | Checks whether the application process is still functioning. |\n\nFor example, `/ready` could verify that the database connection is available.\n\nAvoid making liveness checks depend on every external service. If MongoDB becomes temporarily unavailable, the application process may still be functioning and should not necessarily be restarted repeatedly.\n\n## 5. Understanding Liveness and Readiness\n\nThese are two important concepts in containerized application operations.\n\n### Liveness\n\nLiveness answers:\n\n**Is the application process still functioning?**\n\nA liveness check helps detect applications that have become stuck or stopped making progress.\n\nFor example:\n\nAn API process might still exist but be unable to handle requests because of an internal deadlock.\n\nA failed liveness check can indicate that the process needs to be restarted.\n\n### Readiness\n\nReadiness answers:\n\n**Is the application ready to receive normal traffic?**\n\nAn application might be running but not ready because:\n\n- Database connections are still being established.\n- Application configuration is still loading.\n- Required services are not available.\n- Initial data preparation is incomplete.\n\nA failed readiness check should normally prevent the application from receiving traffic.\n\n### Comparison\n\n| Feature | Liveness | Readiness |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main question | Is it functioning? | Can it accept traffic? |\n| Detects | Stuck or failed application process | Incomplete startup or unavailable dependencies |\n| Typical response | Restart may be appropriate | Stop routing traffic temporarily |\n| Main purpose | Application recovery | Traffic management |\n\nDocker's built-in `HEALTHCHECK` provides one health status. It does not independently implement the full Kubernetes-style distinction between liveness and readiness probes.\n\nThe application can expose separate endpoints, while an orchestrator can monitor them using different policies.\n\n## 6. Monitoring Container Health\n\nOnce a container has a health check, we can inspect its status.\n\nBuild an image:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:1.0 .\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `docker build` creates an image.\n- `-t` assigns a name and tag.\n- `marketplace-api:1.0` identifies the image.\n- `.` specifies the current directory as the build context.\n\nRun it:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nThe command:\n\n- Runs the container in detached mode.\n- Names it `marketplace-api`.\n- Publishes host port 3000 to container port 3000.\n- Uses the image `marketplace-api:1.0`.\n\nCheck its status:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps\n```\n\nYou may see:\n\n```text\nSTATUS\nUp 2 minutes (healthy)\n```\n\nIf the health check fails repeatedly, you may see:\n\n```text\nUp 2 minutes (unhealthy)\n```\n\nInspect the health check history:\n\n```bash\ndocker inspect marketplace-api\n```\n\nLook for the `Health` section under `State`.\n\nYou can also run:\n\n```bash\ndocker inspect --format='{{json .State.Health}}' marketplace-api\n```\n\nThis displays health information, including:\n\n- Current health status.\n- Number of consecutive failures.\n- Recent health check results.\n- Exit codes.\n- Health check output.\n\n### Viewing application logs\n\n```bash\ndocker logs marketplace-api\n```\n\nFollow logs continuously:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs -f marketplace-api\n```\n\nThe `-f` option follows new log entries as they are generated.\n\nLogs help identify why an application is failing its health check.\n\n## 7. Part 1 Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a simple Dockerized Node.js API with:\n\n1. An Express server.\n2. A `/health` endpoint.\n3. A server listening on port 3000.\n4. A Dockerfile with a health check.\n5. A container named `marketplace-api`.\n\nYour tasks:\n\n- Build the image.\n- Start the container.\n- Check its status.\n- Inspect its health check history.\n- View application logs.\n\n**Expected result:** Docker should eventually report the container as healthy.\n\nIf it reports unhealthy, investigate the endpoint, startup time, application logs and health check command.\n\nProgress: Part 1 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 OF 4: CONTAINER MONITORING, DEPENDENCIES AND RELIABILITY\n\n## 8. Why Container Health Is Important in Production\n\nConsider a real marketplace platform with these services:\n\n| Service | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Frontend | Displays the marketplace to customers. |\n| API | Processes application requests. |\n| MongoDB | Stores users, products and transactions. |\n| Redis | Provides caching and temporary data storage. |\n| Worker | Processes background tasks. |\n| Nginx | Handles reverse proxying and traffic routing. |\n\nEach service can experience different failures.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The frontend may work while the API is unavailable.\n- The API may respond while MongoDB is disconnected.\n- Redis may stop responding while the API continues operating.\n- A background worker may stop processing queued tasks.\n\nMonitoring only whether containers are running is insufficient.\n\nWe need to monitor the actual services and their dependencies.\n\n## 9. Container Health and Database Connections\n\nSuppose our marketplace API depends on MongoDB.\n\nThe API may start before MongoDB is ready.\n\nThis can create a startup problem.\n\nImagine:\n\n1. Docker starts MongoDB.\n2. Docker starts the API.\n3. The API immediately attempts a database connection.\n4. MongoDB is still initializing.\n5. The API fails to connect.\n\nThe application must handle this situation properly.\n\nThere are several approaches.\n\n### Approach 1: Application-level connection retries\n\nThe application can attempt to connect repeatedly.\n\nExample:\n\n```javascript\nasync function connectDatabase() {\n    try {\n        await mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGO_URI);\n\n        console.log(\"Database connected\");\n    } catch (error) {\n        console.error(\"Database connection failed\");\n\n        setTimeout(connectDatabase, 5000);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nLine-by-line explanation:\n\n```javascript\nasync function connectDatabase() {\n```\n\nDefines an asynchronous function that connects to MongoDB.\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n```\n\nStarts a block for operations that might fail.\n\n```javascript\nawait mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGO_URI);\n```\n\nAttempts to establish a MongoDB connection using the configured connection string.\n\n`await` pauses this function until the connection succeeds or fails.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(\"Database connected\");\n```\n\nReports successful connection.\n\n```javascript\ncatch (error) {\n```\n\nHandles a connection failure.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.error(\"Database connection failed\");\n```\n\nWrites an error message to the application logs.\n\n```javascript\nsetTimeout(connectDatabase, 5000);\n```\n\nSchedules another connection attempt after five seconds.\n\nThis is a simplified retry implementation. Production systems should also use bounded retries, exponential backoff, appropriate error classification and a clear shutdown policy.\n\n### Approach 2: Docker Compose health dependencies\n\nDocker Compose allows services to declare dependencies.\n\nExample:\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  database:\n    image: mongo:8\n    healthcheck:\n      test:\n        - CMD\n        - mongosh\n        - --quiet\n        - --eval\n        - \"db.adminCommand('ping').ok\"\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n      start_period: 20s\n\n  api:\n    build: .\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    depends_on:\n      database:\n        condition: service_healthy\n```\n\nLet's understand the configuration.\n\n**Services:**\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n```\n\nDefines the containers managed by Docker Compose.\n\n**Database service:**\n\n```yaml\ndatabase:\n  image: mongo:8\n```\n\nCreates a service named `database` using the MongoDB 8 image.\n\n**Database health check:**\n\n```yaml\nhealthcheck:\n```\n\nDefines how Docker Compose checks MongoDB health.\n\n```yaml\ntest:\n  - CMD\n  - mongosh\n  - --quiet\n  - --eval\n  - \"db.adminCommand('ping').ok\"\n```\n\nRuns a MongoDB ping command using the MongoDB shell.\n\nThe result helps determine whether MongoDB can respond to a basic database command.\n\n**Health check timing:**\n\n```yaml\ninterval: 10s\ntimeout: 5s\nretries: 5\nstart_period: 20s\n```\n\nThese settings control how frequently the check runs, how long it can take, how many failures are allowed and the initial startup grace period.\n\n**API service:**\n\n```yaml\napi:\n  build: .\n```\n\nBuilds the API image using the Dockerfile in the current directory.\n\n**Port mapping:**\n\n```yaml\nports:\n  - \"3000:3000\"\n```\n\nPublishes container port 3000 through host port 3000.\n\n**Dependency condition:**\n\n```yaml\ndepends_on:\n  database:\n    condition: service_healthy\n```\n\nTells Compose to wait until the database passes its health check before starting the API.\n\nThis is useful for initial startup ordering.\n\nHowever, it does not guarantee that MongoDB will remain healthy forever. The API must still handle database failures during normal operation.\n\nAlso, the MongoDB image must contain the `mongosh` command, and the health check should be tested against the exact image being deployed.\n\n## 10. Using Docker Compose to Monitor Multiple Services\n\nFor a marketplace application, we may want to monitor both the API and database.\n\nExample:\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: .\n    container_name: marketplace-api\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    environment:\n      MONGO_URI: mongodb://database:27017/marketplace\n    depends_on:\n      database:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    healthcheck:\n      test:\n        - CMD\n        - node\n        - -e\n        - \"fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/health').then(r => process.exit(r.ok ? 0 : 1)).catch(() => process.exit(1))\"\n      interval: 30s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 3\n      start_period: 15s\n\n  database:\n    image: mongo:8\n    healthcheck:\n      test:\n        - CMD\n        - mongosh\n        - --quiet\n        - --eval\n        - \"db.adminCommand('ping').ok\"\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n```\n\nThe important relationship is:\n\n```text\nMarketplace API\n      |\n      v\n   MongoDB\n```\n\nThe API depends on MongoDB.\n\nThe database must pass its health check before Compose starts the API.\n\nNotice that the API connects to:\n\n```text\nmongodb://database:27017/marketplace\n```\n\nThe hostname `database` is the Compose service name.\n\nContainers on the same Compose network can use service names to communicate.\n\nThey do not normally need to use `localhost` to reach another container.\n\nInside the API container, `localhost` refers to the API container itself, not the MongoDB container.\n\n### Starting the application\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d --build\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `docker compose` invokes Compose.\n- `up` creates and starts services.\n- `-d` runs them in the background.\n- `--build` builds images before starting services.\n\n### Checking service status\n\n```bash\ndocker compose ps\n```\n\n### Viewing all service logs\n\n```bash\ndocker compose logs\n```\n\n### Following logs continuously\n\n```bash\ndocker compose logs -f\n```\n\n### Viewing only API logs\n\n```bash\ndocker compose logs -f api\n```\n\n### Stopping the application\n\n```bash\ndocker compose down\n```\n\nThis stops and removes the containers and default network.\n\nIt does not normally remove named volumes unless you explicitly request volume removal.\n\n## 11. Resource Monitoring\n\nA container can be healthy but still consume too many system resources.\n\nFor example:\n\n- An API may consume excessive memory.\n- A background worker may use too much CPU.\n- A memory leak may cause gradual memory growth.\n- Too many containers may overload the VPS.\n\nDocker provides resource monitoring commands.\n\n### CPU and memory usage\n\n```bash\ndocker stats\n```\n\nThis displays live resource usage for running containers.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nCONTAINER       CPU %     MEM USAGE\nmarketplace-api  12.5%     250MiB\nmongo            18.2%     800MiB\nredis             2.1%      90MiB\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- The API uses 12.5% CPU.\n- MongoDB uses 800 MiB of memory.\n- Redis uses 90 MiB of memory.\n\nThese figures are examples, not recommended resource limits.\n\n### Monitor one container\n\n```bash\ndocker stats marketplace-api\n```\n\n### Inspect resource configuration\n\n```bash\ndocker inspect marketplace-api\n```\n\nYou can examine the container's configured resource limits.\n\n### Setting memory and CPU limits\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  --memory=512m \\\n  --cpus=1.0 \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n```bash\n--memory=512m\n```\n\nSets a memory limit of 512 MiB.\n\n```bash\n--cpus=1.0\n```\n\nLimits the container to approximately one CPU core's worth of CPU time.\n\nResource limits protect a server from one container consuming all available resources.\n\nHowever, setting limits too low can cause performance problems or out-of-memory termination.\n\n## 12. Automatic Restart Policies\n\nWhat happens when an application container stops unexpectedly?\n\nDocker provides restart policies.\n\n| Policy | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| `no` | Does not automatically restart the container. |\n| `on-failure` | Restarts after a non-zero exit code. |\n| `always` | Restarts whenever the container stops, subject to Docker daemon behaviour. |\n| `unless-stopped` | Restarts unless the container was explicitly stopped. |\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  --restart unless-stopped \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n```bash\n--restart unless-stopped\n```\n\nConfigures Docker to restart the container unless it has been deliberately stopped.\n\n### Important: Restart policies are not health recovery policies\n\nA restart policy generally responds to the container's main process exiting.\n\nIt does not automatically restart a container just because its health status changes to `unhealthy`.\n\nA running but unhealthy container may continue running.\n\nYou need an orchestrator, monitoring system or additional recovery mechanism if you want automated action based on health-check failures.\n\n## 13. Understanding Container Logs\n\nLogs are essential for diagnosing application problems.\n\nConsider this API log:\n\n```text\nServer started on port 3000\nDatabase connection failed\nRetrying database connection\nDatabase connected\n```\n\nThese messages reveal the application's startup process.\n\nDocker commands:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs marketplace-api\n```\n\nDisplay logs.\n\n```bash\ndocker logs --tail 50 marketplace-api\n```\n\nDisplays the latest 50 lines.\n\n```bash\ndocker logs --since 10m marketplace-api\n```\n\nDisplays logs generated during approximately the last 10 minutes.\n\n```bash\ndocker logs -f marketplace-api\n```\n\nContinuously follows logs.\n\n### Why structured logging matters\n\nProduction applications should produce meaningful and structured logs.\n\nExample JSON log:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"level\": \"error\",\n  \"service\": \"marketplace-api\",\n  \"message\": \"Database connection failed\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-24T10:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nStructured logs make it easier for monitoring systems to search and analyse application events.\n\nNever write passwords, access tokens, private keys or sensitive customer information into logs.\n\n## 14. Container Health Monitoring Tools\n\nLarger deployments may use external monitoring systems.\n\nExamples include:\n\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Prometheus | Collects and stores metrics. |\n| Grafana | Visualizes metrics and dashboards. |\n| cAdvisor | Collects container resource metrics. |\n| OpenTelemetry | Provides instrumentation and telemetry collection. |\n| Loki | Collects and queries logs. |\n| Alertmanager | Handles Prometheus alerts. |\n\nA simplified monitoring architecture:\n\n```text\nContainers\n    |\n    v\nMetrics and Logs\n    |\n    v\nMonitoring System\n    |\n    v\nDashboards and Alerts\n```\n\nFor example, Prometheus may collect CPU usage and application metrics.\n\nGrafana can display those metrics in dashboards.\n\nAn alerting system can notify administrators when memory usage becomes too high or a service stops responding.\n\n## 15. Part 2 Practical Exercise\n\nExtend your marketplace API deployment.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Create an API service.\n2. Create a MongoDB service.\n3. Add a MongoDB health check.\n4. Make the API wait for the database to become healthy during startup.\n5. Add an API health check.\n6. Configure a restart policy.\n7. Set reasonable CPU and memory limits.\n8. Inspect logs and resource usage.\n\nCommands to practise:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d --build\n\ndocker compose ps\n\ndocker compose logs -f api\n\ndocker stats\n\ndocker inspect marketplace-api\n```\n\n**Expected result:** Both services should start successfully, and you should be able to inspect their health and resource usage.\n\nProgress: Part 2 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 OF 4: CONTAINER DELIVERY AND PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT\n\n## 16. What Is Container Delivery?\n\nContainer delivery is the process of taking a containerized application from development and making it available in a target environment.\n\nThe process commonly includes:\n\n1. Writing application code.\n2. Testing the application.\n3. Building a Docker image.\n4. Checking the image for vulnerabilities.\n5. Tagging the image.\n6. Uploading the image to a container registry.\n7. Pulling the image into the deployment environment.\n8. Starting the container.\n9. Checking application health.\n10. Monitoring the deployed service.\n\nThis process allows developers to deliver applications consistently.\n\n### Understanding the delivery pipeline\n\n```text\nDeveloper Writes Code\n         |\n         v\n    Source Control\n         |\n         v\n     Automated Tests\n         |\n         v\n     Docker Build\n         |\n         v\n   Security Scanning\n         |\n         v\n    Image Registry\n         |\n         v\n    Production VPS\n         |\n         v\n   Health Verification\n         |\n         v\n     Live Application\n```\n\nThis is an example of a container delivery pipeline.\n\n## 17. Understanding Container Registries\n\nA container registry is a system for storing and distributing container images.\n\nInstead of manually copying Docker images between computers, developers can upload images to a registry and download them wherever needed.\n\nCommon container registries include:\n\n| Registry | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Docker Hub | Public and private Docker image hosting. |\n| GitHub Container Registry | Container image hosting integrated with GitHub. |\n| Amazon ECR | AWS container registry. |\n| Azure Container Registry | Microsoft's managed container registry. |\n| Google Artifact Registry | Google's artifact and container registry. |\n\nFor our practical example, we will use Docker Hub.\n\nOfficial registry: https://hub.docker.com/\n\n## 18. Tagging Container Images\n\nA Docker image needs an identifiable name and tag.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:1.0 .\n```\n\nThe image is named:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api\n```\n\nIts version tag is:\n\n```text\n1.0\n```\n\nA more complete image reference can look like:\n\n```text\nusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nfireboxuser/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `fireboxuser` is the registry namespace.\n- `marketplace-api` is the image repository.\n- `1.0` is the image tag.\n\n### Why version tags matter\n\nImagine you deploy version 1.0 and later release version 1.1.\n\nYou should be able to identify which version is running.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api:1.0\nmarketplace-api:1.1\nmarketplace-api:1.2\n```\n\nThis makes deployment tracking and rollback easier.\n\nFor production, avoid depending entirely on the `latest` tag.\n\nA tag can be moved to point to a different image. For stronger reproducibility, use immutable version tags and record image digests.\n\nA digest identifies a specific image content.\n\n## 19. Uploading an Image to Docker Hub\n\nFirst, build the image:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:1.0 .\n```\n\nTag it with your Docker Hub username:\n\n```bash\ndocker tag marketplace-api:1.0 yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nReplace `yourusername` with your actual Docker Hub username.\n\nLog in:\n\n```bash\ndocker login\n```\n\nDocker requests your registry authentication.\n\nFor automated environments, prefer an access token or securely configured credential mechanism instead of putting a password directly into a command.\n\nPush the image:\n\n```bash\ndocker push yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nDocker uploads the image layers to the registry.\n\nOnce uploaded, the image can be downloaded on another machine.\n\n## 20. Pulling and Running an Image on a VPS\n\nSuppose you have a VPS running Linux.\n\nYou want to deploy the marketplace API on that server.\n\nFirst, ensure Docker is installed and running.\n\nThen download the image:\n\n```bash\ndocker pull yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nThis retrieves the image from the registry.\n\nRun the container:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  --restart unless-stopped \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nThe VPS now runs the marketplace API container.\n\nCheck the container:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps\n```\n\nCheck the logs:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs marketplace-api\n```\n\nTest the endpoint locally on the VPS:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:3000/health\n```\n\nExpected response:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"healthy\",\n  \"service\": \"marketplace-api\"\n}\n```\n\nIf the API is intended to be publicly accessible, you must also configure firewall rules, reverse proxying and HTTPS appropriately.\n\nDo not expose databases or internal services directly to the public internet unless there is a specific, secured reason to do so.\n\n## 21. Deploying Updates\n\nSuppose you have made changes to your marketplace API.\n\nYou now need to deliver version 1.1.\n\nThe process is:\n\n### Step 1: Build the new image\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:1.1 .\n```\n\n### Step 2: Tag the image\n\n```bash\ndocker tag marketplace-api:1.1 yourusername/marketplace-api:1.1\n```\n\n### Step 3: Push the new image\n\n```bash\ndocker push yourusername/marketplace-api:1.1\n```\n\n### Step 4: Connect to your VPS\n\nUse your normal secure SSH access method.\n\n### Step 5: Pull the new image\n\n```bash\ndocker pull yourusername/marketplace-api:1.1\n```\n\n### Step 6: Stop the old container\n\n```bash\ndocker stop marketplace-api\n```\n\n### Step 7: Remove the old container\n\n```bash\ndocker rm marketplace-api\n```\n\n### Step 8: Start the new version\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  --restart unless-stopped \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  yourusername/marketplace-api:1.1\n```\n\n### Step 9: Verify the deployment\n\n```bash\ndocker ps\n```\n\n```bash\ndocker logs marketplace-api\n```\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:3000/health\n```\n\nThis is a basic replacement deployment.\n\n**Important:** This approach may cause downtime while the old container is stopped and the new one starts.\n\nFor applications that require minimal downtime, use a reverse proxy with multiple application instances, rolling deployment strategies or an orchestration platform.\n\nAlso, do not remove persistent database volumes when replacing application containers.\n\n## 22. Understanding Rollback\n\nA deployment can introduce problems.\n\nImagine:\n\n- Version 1.0 works correctly.\n- You deploy version 1.1.\n- Users begin receiving errors.\n- The new application cannot process requests properly.\n\nYou may need to return to version 1.0.\n\nThis is called a rollback.\n\nIf the old image is still available, you can run it again:\n\n```bash\ndocker pull yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nStop and remove the problematic container:\n\n```bash\ndocker stop marketplace-api\n\ndocker rm marketplace-api\n```\n\nStart the previous version:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  --restart unless-stopped \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nTest it:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:3000/health\n```\n\n### Important production consideration\n\nRolling back an application image does not automatically reverse database schema changes.\n\nFor example, version 1.1 might have changed a database field or removed an old collection.\n\nVersion 1.0 may no longer work with that modified database.\n\nProduction database migrations should therefore be designed with compatibility and rollback considerations in mind.\n\n## 23. Understanding CI/CD for Container Delivery\n\nCI/CD stands for:\n\n- Continuous Integration.\n- Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment.\n\n### Continuous Integration (CI)\n\nDevelopers frequently submit code changes to a shared repository.\n\nAn automated pipeline can:\n\n- Install dependencies.\n- Run tests.\n- Check code quality.\n- Build the application.\n- Build the Docker image.\n- Scan the image for vulnerabilities.\n\nThe objective is to identify problems early.\n\n### Continuous Delivery\n\nThe application is prepared for deployment.\n\nThe image is built, tested and stored in a registry.\n\nA release can then be deployed through an approved process.\n\n### Continuous Deployment\n\nSuccessful changes can automatically progress into production when all required checks pass.\n\nThis requires appropriate automated testing, security controls and deployment safeguards.\n\n## 24. Example: GitHub Actions Docker Build Pipeline\n\nGitHub Actions can automate container builds.\n\nCreate this file in your repository:\n\n```text\n.github/workflows/docker.yml\n```\n\nExample workflow:\n\n```yaml\nname: Build Marketplace API\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout source code\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Set up Docker Buildx\n        uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3\n\n      - name: Login to Docker Hub\n        uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with:\n          username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_TOKEN }}\n\n      - name: Build and push image\n        uses: docker/build-push-action@v6\n        with:\n          context: .\n          push: true\n          tags: yourusername/marketplace-api:latest\n```\n\nLet's examine the workflow.\n\n**Workflow name:**\n\n```yaml\nname: Build Marketplace API\n```\n\nNames the automated workflow.\n\n**Trigger:**\n\n```yaml\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n```\n\nRuns the workflow whenever code is pushed to the `main` branch.\n\n**Job:**\n\n```yaml\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n```\n\nCreates a job named `build` that runs on an Ubuntu environment.\n\n**Checkout:**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Checkout source code\n  uses: actions/checkout@v4\n```\n\nDownloads the repository contents into the workflow environment.\n\n**Docker Buildx:**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Set up Docker Buildx\n  uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3\n```\n\nPrepares Docker Buildx for building container images.\n\n**Registry login:**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Login to Docker Hub\n  uses: docker/login-action@v3\n```\n\nAuthenticates the workflow with Docker Hub.\n\n**Credentials:**\n\n```yaml\nusername: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}\npassword: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_TOKEN }}\n```\n\nReads credentials from GitHub repository secrets.\n\nThese secrets must be configured in the repository's settings.\n\nNever write real passwords or tokens directly into workflow files.\n\n**Build and push:**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Build and push image\n  uses: docker/build-push-action@v6\n```\n\nUses an action to build and publish the Docker image.\n\n```yaml\ncontext: .\n```\n\nUses the repository root as the build context.\n\n```yaml\npush: true\n```\n\nUploads the image to the registry.\n\n```yaml\ntags: yourusername/marketplace-api:latest\n```\n\nAssigns an image tag.\n\nReplace `yourusername` with your actual Docker Hub namespace.\n\n### Production improvement\n\nA production pipeline should normally publish a unique release tag or commit-based tag instead of relying only on `latest`.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nyourusername/marketplace-api:1.2.0\nyourusername/marketplace-api:commit-a1b2c3d\n```\n\nThis makes it easier to identify and deploy the exact image produced by a particular release.\n\n## 25. Container Image Security Before Delivery\n\nA successful Docker build does not guarantee that an image is secure.\n\nBefore deploying an image, examine its security.\n\nImportant practices include:\n\n### 1. Use trusted base images\n\nAvoid downloading random images from unknown publishers.\n\nUse maintained official or trusted images.\n\n### 2. Update dependencies\n\nOutdated packages can contain known vulnerabilities.\n\nRegularly update dependencies and rebuild images.\n\n### 3. Scan images\n\nTools such as Trivy can scan images for known vulnerabilities.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ntrivy image yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nThis examines the image for known security issues.\n\n### 4. Run as a non-root user\n\nAvoid running application processes as root unless required.\n\nDockerfile example:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN addgroup -S appgroup && adduser -S appuser -G appgroup\n\nUSER appuser\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN addgroup -S appgroup\n```\n\nCreates a system group named `appgroup`.\n\n```dockerfile\nadduser -S appuser -G appgroup\n```\n\nCreates a system user and associates it with the group.\n\n```dockerfile\nUSER appuser\n```\n\nSets the user used by subsequent container instructions and the default application process.\n\nThis example uses Alpine Linux commands. Debian-based images use different user and group management commands.\n\n### 5. Protect secrets\n\nDo not include these in Dockerfiles or image layers:\n\n- Database passwords.\n- API keys.\n- Private keys.\n- Authentication tokens.\n- Production credentials.\n\nUse secure runtime configuration and secrets management.\n\n### 6. Keep images small\n\nSmaller images can reduce transfer time and unnecessary packages.\n\nMulti-stage builds and `.dockerignore` files help reduce image size.\n\n### 7. Consider image provenance and integrity\n\nFor more mature delivery pipelines, consider:\n\n- Generating a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM).\n- Signing images.\n- Verifying image signatures before deployment.\n- Recording image digests.\n- Restricting registry permissions.\n\nAn SBOM lists the software components included in an image.\n\nImage signing helps establish that an image came from an expected source and has not been altered.\n\n## 26. Understanding Deployment Strategies\n\nDifferent applications require different deployment methods.\n\n### Recreate deployment\n\nThe old application is stopped before the new application starts.\n\n```text\nOld Version Stops\n        |\n        v\nNew Version Starts\n```\n\nAdvantages:\n- Simple.\n- Easy to implement.\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Can cause downtime.\n\n### Rolling deployment\n\nInstances are updated gradually.\n\n```text\nInstance 1: Updated\nInstance 2: Old Version\nInstance 3: Old Version\n```\n\nThe process continues until all instances run the new version.\n\nAdvantages:\n- Can reduce downtime.\n- Allows gradual replacement.\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Requires compatibility between versions during deployment.\n\n### Blue-green deployment\n\nTwo environments are maintained:\n\n- Blue: current production version.\n- Green: new version.\n\nAfter testing the green environment, traffic is switched to it.\n\n```text\n          Traffic\n             |\n             v\n       Reverse Proxy\n         /       \\\n        /         \\\n     Blue         Green\n   Version 1    Version 2\n```\n\nIf the new version fails, traffic can be redirected to the previous environment.\n\nThis approach requires additional resources because both environments may need to operate simultaneously.\n\n## 27. Container Delivery on a VPS\n\nA common architecture for a production application is:\n\n```text\n             Internet\n                 |\n                 v\n              Nginx\n                 |\n                 v\n          Marketplace API\n                 |\n          +------+------+\n          |             |\n          v             v\n       MongoDB         Redis\n```\n\nNginx acts as a reverse proxy.\n\nIt receives incoming HTTP or HTTPS requests and forwards appropriate traffic to the API.\n\nThe API communicates with MongoDB and Redis over an internal Docker network.\n\nFor a production VPS, consider:\n\n- Firewall configuration.\n- HTTPS certificates.\n- Secure SSH access.\n- Container health checks.\n- Resource limits.\n- Persistent database storage.\n- Automated backups.\n- Centralized logs.\n- Monitoring and alerts.\n- Secure environment variables.\n- A tested rollback process.\n\nA container is not a complete production deployment by itself. The surrounding infrastructure and operational procedures are equally important.\n\n## 28. Part 3 Practical Project: Deliver a Marketplace API\n\nYour task is to create a complete container delivery workflow.\n\n### Stage 1: Development\n\nCreate a Node.js Express API.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- `/health` endpoint.\n- `/products` endpoint.\n- MongoDB connection.\n- Appropriate error handling.\n\n### Stage 2: Containerization\n\nCreate a Dockerfile.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Use a maintained Node.js base image.\n- Install dependencies.\n- Copy application files.\n- Expose the correct application port.\n- Add a health check.\n- Run as a non-root user where practical.\n\n### Stage 3: Local deployment\n\nBuild and run the image.\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:1.0 .\n```\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nTest:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:3000/health\n```\n\n### Stage 4: Registry delivery\n\nTag the image:\n\n```bash\ndocker tag marketplace-api:1.0 yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nPush it:\n\n```bash\ndocker push yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\n### Stage 5: VPS deployment\n\nPull the image:\n\n```bash\ndocker pull yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0\n```\n\nRun it on the VPS.\n\nVerify the health endpoint.\n\n### Stage 6: Release management\n\nBuild version 1.1.\n\nPush it to the registry.\n\nDeploy it and verify that it works.\n\nPractise rolling back to version 1.0.\n\n### Stage 7: Automation\n\nCreate a GitHub Actions workflow that builds and pushes your Docker image whenever code is pushed to the main branch.\n\n**Project objective:** Demonstrate that you can develop, containerize, test, publish, deploy, monitor and update an application.\n\nProgress: Part 3 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 OF 4: KAI ASSESSMENT \u2014 CONTAINER HEALTH AND DELIVERY\n\n## 29. Assessment Instructions\n\nKai will evaluate your understanding of:\n\n- Container lifecycle and health.\n- Docker health checks.\n- Liveness and readiness.\n- Docker Compose dependencies.\n- Resource monitoring.\n- Restart policies.\n- Container registries.\n- Image tagging and delivery.\n- CI/CD automation.\n- Production deployment.\n- Security and rollback.\n\nAnswer the questions carefully.\n\nFor practical questions, explain the commands and their purposes instead of simply copying them.\n\n## SECTION A: MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 1**\n\nWhat does a Docker health check do?\n\nA. Automatically increases container memory.\n\nB. Checks whether an application inside a container is functioning as expected.\n\nC. Builds a new Docker image.\n\nD. Uploads images to Docker Hub.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nCan a Docker container be running but unhealthy?\n\nA. No.\n\nB. Yes.\n\nC. Only when using MongoDB.\n\nD. Only when using Kubernetes.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nWhich Dockerfile instruction defines a health check?\n\nA. `EXPOSE`\n\nB. `HEALTHCHECK`\n\nC. `RUN`\n\nD. `VOLUME`\n\n**Question 4**\n\nWhat does a readiness check determine?\n\nA. Whether an application can receive normal traffic.\n\nB. Whether the image has been uploaded.\n\nC. Whether the Docker daemon is installed.\n\nD. Whether a container has been deleted.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nWhich command displays live CPU and memory usage for containers?\n\nA. `docker images`\n\nB. `docker stats`\n\nC. `docker build`\n\nD. `docker tag`\n\n**Question 6**\n\nWhich command uploads an image to Docker Hub?\n\nA. `docker pull`\n\nB. `docker start`\n\nC. `docker push`\n\nD. `docker inspect`\n\n**Question 7**\n\nWhat does the restart policy `unless-stopped` mean?\n\nA. The container can never restart.\n\nB. Docker restarts the container unless it has been explicitly stopped.\n\nC. The container restarts every 10 seconds.\n\nD. Docker deletes the container after failure.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nWhat is the purpose of a container registry?\n\nA. To store and distribute container images.\n\nB. To monitor CPU temperature.\n\nC. To write application code.\n\nD. To create database tables.\n\n## SECTION B: SHORT-ANSWER QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 9**\n\nExplain the difference between a running container and a healthy container.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nWhat is a Docker health check, and why is it important in production?\n\n**Question 11**\n\nDifferentiate between liveness and readiness checks.\n\n**Question 12**\n\nWhy should a marketplace API handle MongoDB connection failures even when Docker Compose uses `depends_on` with `condition: service_healthy`?\n\n**Question 13**\n\nExplain the purpose of each of these health check settings:\n\n- `interval`\n- `timeout`\n- `start_period`\n- `retries`\n\n**Question 14**\n\nWhat is container resource monitoring, and why should developers monitor CPU and memory usage?\n\n**Question 15**\n\nExplain the difference between Continuous Integration, Continuous Delivery and Continuous Deployment.\n\n## SECTION C: PRACTICAL DOCKER COMMANDS\n\n**Question 16**\n\nWrite a command that builds a Docker image named:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api:2.0\n```\n\n**Question 17**\n\nWrite a command that starts a container with:\n\n- Name: `marketplace-api`\n- Host port: `3000`\n- Container port: `3000`\n- Restart policy: `unless-stopped`\n- Image: `marketplace-api:2.0`\n\n**Question 18**\n\nWrite a command that checks the health status of a container named `marketplace-api`.\n\n**Question 19**\n\nWrite a command that displays the latest 50 lines of logs from the marketplace API container.\n\n**Question 20**\n\nWrite a command that displays live resource usage for the marketplace API container.\n\n**Question 21**\n\nExplain how to tag and push the following image to Docker Hub:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api:2.0\n```\n\nUse `yourusername` as the placeholder registry username.\n\n**Question 22**\n\nWrite the command that downloads this image from Docker Hub:\n\n```text\nyourusername/marketplace-api:2.0\n```\n\n## SECTION D: TROUBLESHOOTING AND SCENARIO QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 23**\n\nYour container reports:\n\n```text\nUp 5 minutes (unhealthy)\n```\n\nWhat does this mean?\n\nIdentify at least four possible causes and explain how you would investigate them.\n\n**Question 24**\n\nYour marketplace API starts before MongoDB is ready and fails to connect.\n\nExplain two ways to solve this problem.\n\n**Question 25**\n\nYour API container is consuming 95% of available server memory.\n\nExplain the steps you would take to investigate and control the problem.\n\n**Question 26**\n\nYou deploy version 1.1 of your API, but users begin experiencing errors.\n\nExplain how you would roll back to version 1.0.\n\n**Question 27**\n\nYour application works on your development computer but fails on your production VPS.\n\nIdentify at least five possible causes.\n\n**Question 28**\n\nA Docker container has a restart policy of `unless-stopped`, but it remains running even though its health status is `unhealthy`.\n\nExplain why this can happen.\n\n**Question 29**\n\nYour Docker image contains a database password inside its Dockerfile.\n\nExplain why this is dangerous and describe a safer approach.\n\n**Question 30**\n\nYou want to deploy a new version of your API without stopping all existing instances at once.\n\nWhich deployment strategy could you use, and how does it work?\n\n## SECTION E: FINAL PRACTICAL ASSESSMENT\n\n### Project: Production Container Health and Delivery System\n\nBuild a production-style container delivery workflow for a marketplace API.\n\nYour system must contain:\n\n| Component | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| API | Node.js Express application |\n| Database | MongoDB |\n| Containerization | Dockerfile |\n| Health monitoring | Docker health checks |\n| Service coordination | Docker Compose |\n| Resource management | CPU and memory limits |\n| Restart behaviour | Appropriate restart policy |\n| Registry | Docker Hub or another registry |\n| Automation | GitHub Actions |\n| Deployment | Linux VPS |\n| Recovery | Rollback procedure |\n\n### Project tasks\n\n1. Build the marketplace API.\n2. Create a working health endpoint.\n3. Write a Dockerfile with a health check.\n4. Create a Docker Compose configuration for the API and MongoDB.\n5. Configure database health monitoring.\n6. Test the application locally.\n7. Monitor container logs.\n8. Inspect CPU and memory usage.\n9. Build and tag a release image.\n10. Push the image to a container registry.\n11. Deploy it on a Linux VPS.\n12. Configure HTTPS through a reverse proxy.\n13. Create a GitHub Actions build workflow.\n14. Release a second application version.\n15. Demonstrate a rollback to the previous version.\n\n### Project evaluation\n\n| Assessment area | Marks |\n|---|---:|\n| Dockerfile and image building | 10 |\n| Health checks and monitoring | 15 |\n| Docker Compose configuration | 10 |\n| Resource and restart management | 10 |\n| Registry and image delivery | 15 |\n| CI/CD automation | 15 |\n| VPS deployment | 10 |\n| Security and rollback | 15 |\n| **Total** | **100** |\n\n## 30. Lesson Completion Checklist\n\nBefore completing this lesson, make sure you can:\n\n- [ ] Explain Docker container health.\n- [ ] Distinguish between running and healthy containers.\n- [ ] Create a Docker health check.\n- [ ] Build a working application health endpoint.\n- [ ] Explain liveness and readiness.\n- [ ] Configure Docker Compose health dependencies.\n- [ ] Monitor container logs.\n- [ ] Monitor CPU and memory usage.\n- [ ] Configure restart policies.\n- [ ] Build and tag Docker images.\n- [ ] Push images to a registry.\n- [ ] Pull images onto a VPS.\n- [ ] Explain CI/CD.\n- [ ] Automate image builds using GitHub Actions.\n- [ ] Understand container image security.\n- [ ] Deploy new application versions.\n- [ ] Perform a rollback.\n\n## 31. Final Submission to Kai\n\nSubmit your answers to Questions 1\u201330 and your practical project.\n\nKai should assess:\n\n1. Technical accuracy.\n2. Understanding of container health.\n3. Correct Docker commands.\n4. Ability to troubleshoot failures.\n5. Understanding of production delivery.\n6. Practical deployment skills.\n7. Security awareness.\n\nKai should identify incorrect answers, explain the correct reasoning and recommend additional practice where necessary.\n\n**Part 4 of 4 \u2014 Lesson Complete**\n\n**Next recommended lesson:**\n\n### Docker and Containers: Networking and Container Communication";
